Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!test_and_clear_bit(NCI_UP, &ndev->flags)) {
	// ... flush and timers ...
	mutex_unlock(&ndev->req_lock);
	return 0;
}
```
and
```
	clear_bit(NCI_INIT, &ndev->flags);
	flush_workqueue(ndev->cmd_wq);
	timer_delete_sync(&ndev->cmd_timer);
	// No completion before clearing flags
	ndev->flags &= BIT(NCI_UNREG);
	mutex_unlock(&ndev->req_lock);
	return 0;
```
Reason:
When `nci_close_device()` is called, if the device is already down (`NCI_UP` cleared) or after resetting the device, any pending data exchange (indicated by `NCI_DATA_EXCHANGE` flag) is not completed. The data exchange callback (e.g., `rawsock_data_exchange_complete`) holds a socket reference that is only released when the callback is invoked. Without calling `nci_data_exchange_complete()`, the socket reference persists, causing a memory leak as shown in the kernel memory trace.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+		if (test_bit(NCI_DATA_EXCHANGE, &ndev->flags))
+			nci_data_exchange_complete(ndev, NULL,
+						   ndev->cur_conn_id,
+						   -ENODEV);
```
and
```
+	timer_delete_sync(&ndev->data_timer);
+
+	if (test_bit(NCI_DATA_EXCHANGE, &ndev->flags))
+		nci_data_exchange_complete(ndev, NULL, ndev->cur_conn_id,
+					   -ENODEV);
```
Reason:
The patch adds calls to `nci_data_exchange_complete()` in two places within `nci_close_device()`: one in the early return path when `NCI_UP` is not set, and another after resetting the device and cleaning up timers. These calls ensure that any pending data exchange is aborted with an `-ENODEV` status, which triggers the release of the socket reference held by the callback. This prevents the memory leak by properly completing the exchange before the device is closed.

